WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.140
An impenetrable, physical, tall, powerful,
beautiful, southern border wall.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.860
Should we build the wall?

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:17.200
They're bringing drugs, they're bringing crime,
they're rapists

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.800
and some, I assume are good people.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.640
Donald Trump recently traveled to the US/Mexico
border near San Diego

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.940
to tour prototypes of the big beautiful wall

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.560
that was the centerpiece oh his presidential campaign.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:33.000
Trump supporters came out to express their
enthusiasm.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.480
Why do you support the wall?

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:41.380
It's like, if you had a kennel, with dogs,

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.700
I'm not saying that we're all dogs or anything like that but,

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.780
are we going to let them all run in the field?

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.280
Trump has requested 25 billion dollars just
to build the wall

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.980
and independent estimates put the price tag
even higher.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:57.200
Trump's saving a lot of money so, nobody should
be complaining about the cost.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.660
Everything that comes with illegals and drugs
and other things, that's also expensive.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.540
Annual maintenance would run at least hundreds
of millions of dollars

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:10.100
on top of the billions already being spent
to expand border patrol personnel and technology.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.640
Mexico will pay for the wall.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:14.720
Actually it won't.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.780
I'm not going to pay for that fucking wall.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.960
Which leaves american taxpayers on the hook.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:24.220
There's a lot of people here who are criminals,
drug dealers, gang members.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:29.000
We've seen example after example of people
killing people and then nothing's happening.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.640
But there are good people too,

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.620
so we also have to make sure that we're cracking down on the bad

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:34.920
rather than the good.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.760
Do you think that there's any merit to the
argument

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.600
that instead of spending, you know, 25 billion
dollars on a wall

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.340
we could better use those resources if we're
actually just targeting the bad actors.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:47.260
This is an investment on it's own, any investment's
gonna cost money.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.700
And of course the initial investment's gonna
be huge.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.240
As a border patrol agent, I can tell you that
walls do work.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.620
The US has erected hundreds of miles of fencing
and other barriers in the last 30 years,

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.820
more than quintupled the number of border
patrol agents on the ground

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:06.369
in addition to installing high tech sensors
and cameras and using military drones to patrol

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:09.750
and yet coyotes and drug cartels have out-innovated
authorities

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:15.400
using tunnels, planes, drones, ships, catapults,
car ramps, and even specially built submarines.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.200
So how would this new wall be different?

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.820
You're dealing with people who will find a
way around it,

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.760
but I think one of the most powerful things
we can do is create deterrence.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.000
You build a 30 foot wall, you need a 31 foot
ladder, right?

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.410
Like, what do you say to people that say that?

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.420
They got to come over the other side.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.840
What about if they have a rope?

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.540
I'm in favor regardless of what fence, there
needs to be razor wire,

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.960
but they're staring at a wall of razor wire,
they can't cross.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:44.240
Something concrete or also you're not having
people digging holes under the wall either

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.220
because people can dig holes under walls too.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:50.440
It doesn't have to solve all our problems
but we have to start moving towards a solution

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.120
and I don't think you can have it without
a wall.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.180
But would a new physical wall actually accomplish
what supporters hope for?

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.180
The majority of unauthorized immigrants to
the US don't walk across the border.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.020
They show up with temporary visas and then
overstay them.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.500
And these days more Mexican migrants have
been exiting the country than coming in.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.420
This is the land of the American dream and
we want people to come in here.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:11.960
But we want people to do it the right way,

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.380
like my family did when they came from Mexico and Cuba.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.560
But for Mexican immigrants getting a visa
is nearly impossible.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.340
In 2018 the quota is just 25 thousand people

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:22.930
and there are nearly 1.3 million on the waiting
list.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:28.120
I don't want our country to change to the
point where it is not what it is now.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.000
You're from Iraq, right?

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.680
So people who are pro wall, pro Trump, might
say

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.120
'well people from Iraq are changing the nature
of our country.' right?

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.940
So do you worry if we put caps on legal immigration
that people like you

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:43.060
who are immigrating and trying to do the right
thing won't have that opportunity?

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:46.340
Ok, you gotta care about American citizens
first,

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:51.980
yes there is a lot of people in my original
country that want to come to the United States,

00:03:51.980 --> 00:04:00.200
but I'm an American right now, it's, you gotta
look at what for the American people first.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:04.460
Ironically, expanding fencing and reducing
guest workers over the last few decades

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.700
has led to more unauthorized immigrants staying
in the US.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.700
Because the barriers didn't stop entrance,

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.560
but they did raise the cost of going back over the border.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.800
An exhaustive study by Princeton sociologist
Douglass Massey

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.900
concluded there are now over 5 million more
illegal residents

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.300
than there would have been if policies under
Ronald Reagan had been maintained.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.520
Rather than talking about putting up a fence,

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.880
make it possible for them to come here legally
with a work permit,

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:31.880
and then while they're working and earning
here, they pay taxes here.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.220
And when they want to go back they can go
back, and they can cross,

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:36.240
and open the border both ways.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.220
Only about a third of border land is owned
by the federal government

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:43.300
with the rest split between states, Native
American tribes and private land owners.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:47.120
There's people along the border that would
have to have part of their property taken,

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.640
do you think that, are you concerned about
that?

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.820
I'm not concerned about it, maybe the ranchers
might be.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:57.140
Right there you're all of a sudden going to
that land grabbing thing.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:58.720
It would require some takings, right?

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:00.780
Innocent Americans would be caught up in immigration.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.380
It's the art of the deal, you know?

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:05.160
It's, you know, people want change, people
want change.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.439
Building a wall along the entire 2 thousand
mile border also poses practical challenges.

00:05:10.439 --> 00:05:14.909
A double barrier, like those that already
exist near San Diego and around the Rio Grande

00:05:14.909 --> 00:05:19.300
needs to be porous so that flood waters can
escape and Americans can access their land.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:23.260
If we can build the atomic bomb in in three
or four years we can build a fence.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:25.540
What year was the wall of China built?

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:28.040
There's a lot of walls around the world.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.780
But Trump's proposed wall presents unique
challenges.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.040
In a world in which most illegal immigrants
don't walk across the border

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:36.040
and not a single terrorist ever has,

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.659
in which small businesses rely on immigrant
labor

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.420
and in which average Americans can be hurt
by increased prices

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.599
or violations of their civil liberties

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.600
does it make sense to spend tens of billions
of dollars on this particular wall?

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:52.600
It's at least a symbol that we are willing
to define where our land ends

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:54.160
and, you know, Mexico begins.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.420
America needs to be America first like, point
blank.

